From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:23:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 23/77] Type check the negated constant created by the fastmath
 reassociation rules

The fastmath rules for a+c1-c2 and a-c1+c2 wrap the constant in a new
unary minus node inside a copied, already typed tree, so nothing type
checks it. In inline or constant propagation simplification the always-on
rule left+(-right) => left-right turns it back and the two rules loop
forever (an inline Result := (A - B) + C called with constants hangs at
-O2 -OoFASTMATH, -O3 -OoFASTMATH and -O4); through constant propagation
the untyped node reaches code generation and crashes instead.
---
 compiler/nadd.pas | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/compiler/nadd.pas b/compiler/nadd.pas
index 72a60666..3d06eb22 100644
--- a/compiler/nadd.pas
+++ b/compiler/nadd.pas
@@ -1725,6 +1725,11 @@ const
                     Result:=getcopy;
                     Result.nodetype:=addn;
                     taddnode(result).right:=cunaryminusnode.create(taddnode(result).right);
+                    { fold the negation into the constant right away: the copied tree keeps its
+                      resultdef, so nothing else type checks the new node; left alone, simplify
+                      in inline/constprop context turns a+(-const) back into a-const and loops,
+                      or the untyped node reaches code generation }
+                    do_typecheckpass(taddnode(result).right);
                     exit;
                   end;
 
@@ -1737,6 +1742,8 @@ const
                     Result:=getcopy;
                     taddnode(Result).left.nodetype:=addn;
                     taddnode(taddnode(Result).left).right:=cunaryminusnode.create(taddnode(taddnode(Result).left).right);
+                    { see above }
+                    do_typecheckpass(taddnode(taddnode(Result).left).right);
                     exit;
                   end;
 
